/*  run_object_alias_use_sites — `object X as Y` must emit Y at EVERY use site, not just some.

    The declaration always emitted `object lamp`, and property reads/writes went through
    qualifyIdentifier and correctly emitted `lamp.wattage`. Three receiver positions did not:

      - a method call (`beacon.flash()`), because the single-hop rewrite only fired when the
        qualified name was itself a registered object — which an `as` alias never is, the registry
        keying on the Beguile name;
      - `give`/`has`, which route through the same method-call machinery;
      - a world-tree store (`beacon.parent = …`), where $self is forced to the owner path and that
        path was the Beguile name, so parentProp's `move $self to $v` addressed `beacon`.

    Each emitted a dangling `beacon` that Inform 6 cannot link, so this tier — which runs the
    program — is the one that catches it. A local named the same as an object must still win, which
    is why the fix goes through qualifyIdentifier rather than looking the object up directly.  */
// EXPECT_OUTPUT: watt=4 flashed=1 has=Y moved=Y local=7
#beguilerSettings { target = z5; informName = "none"; }

attribute lit;
object holder as crate { }
object beacon as lamp {
    int wattage;
    int flashed;
    void flash(){ flashed = 1; }
}

void main(){
    beacon.wattage = 4;                  // property write
    print("watt="); print(beacon.wattage);

    beacon.flash();                      // method call on an aliased receiver
    print(" flashed="); print(beacon.flashed);

    beacon.give(lit);                    // give on an aliased receiver
    print(" has="); if(beacon.has(lit)) print("Y"); else print("N");

    beacon.parent = holder;              // world-tree store, aliased owner AND aliased target
    print(" moved="); if(beacon.parent == holder) print("Y"); else print("N");

    // A local may share an object's name; it must shadow it, not pick up the alias.
    int beaconCount = 7;
    print(" local="); print(beaconCount);
}
